        "title": "What is personality?",
        "text": "A  We are all familiar with the idea that different people have different personalities, but what does this actually mean? It suggests that different people behave in different ways, but it must be more than that. After all, different people find themselves in different circumstances, and much of their behaviour follows from this. However, our everyday experience shows that different people respond in remarkably different ways even when they face roughly the same circumstances. Alan might be happy to live alone in a quiet, orderly cottage, go out once a week and stay in the same job for thirty years, while Beth likes nothing better than exotic travel and being surrounded by lively friends and loud music.\n\nB  In cases like these, we feel that it cannot just be the situation that is producing the differences in behaviour. Something about the way the person is 'wired up' seems to be at work, deciding how they react to situations and, more than that, what kind of situations they get themselves into in the first place. This is why personality seems to become stronger as we get older. When we are young, our situation reflects outside factors such as the social and family environment we were born into. As we grow older, we are more and more affected by the results of our own choices (doing jobs that we were drawn to, surrounded by people like us whom we have looked for). So personality differences that might have been very small at birth become dramatic later in adulthood.\n\nC  Personality, then, seems to be the set of lasting, stable tendencies that characterise a person. These tendencies come partly from the expression of inborn features of the nervous system, and partly from learning. Researchers sometimes make a distinction between temperament, which refers only to characteristics that are inborn or directly caused by biological factors, and personality, which also includes social and cultural learning. Nervousness, for example, might be a factor of temperament, but religious belief is an aspect of personality.\n\nD  The discovery that differences in temperament are real is one of the major findings of modern psychology. It could easily have been the case that there were no built-in differences in temperament between people, so that, given the same learning history and the same dilemmas, they would all respond in much the same way. Yet we now know that this is not the case.\n\nE  Personality measures turn out to be good predictors of your health, how happy you usually are — even your taste in paintings. Personality is a much better predictor of these things than social class or age. The origin of these differences is partly innate. That is to say, when people are adopted at birth and brought up by new families, their personalities are more similar to those of their blood relatives than to those of the people they grew up with.\n\nF  Personality differences tend to show themselves through the quick, gut-feeling, intuitive and emotional systems of the human mind. The slower, rational, deliberate systems vary less in output from person to person. Deliberate rational strategies can be used to override intuitive patterns of response, and this is how people who wish to change their personalities or feelings have to go about it. As human beings, we have the unique ability to look at our personality from the outside and decide what we want to do with it.\n\nG  So what are the main ways in which personalities can differ? The dominant approach is to think of the space of possible personalities as being defined by a number of dimensions. Each person can be given a position in this space according to their scores on all the different dimensions. Almost all theories agree on two of the main dimensions, neuroticism (or negative emotionality) and extroversion (or positive emotionality). However, they disagree about how many more dimensions there are. Among the most influential proposals are openness, conscientiousness and agreeableness. In the next section I shall examine these five dimensions."
